  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and mild weather will continue until late \nWednesday or Wednesday night when we'll likely see some rain \nshowers followed by slightly cooler weather.\n\nLatest satellite images, observations and high res HREF \nguidance all show light wind and variable amounts of mid and \nhigh clouds.\n\nThis should remain the case for the est of today with almost no\nlow clouds/cu. More moisture streams into the SW zones of the \nCWA Monday. A sprinkle isn't out of the question over the N \nMonday night - much like the very small and brief shower they \nhad two nights ago. We'll keep the forecast at 0 PoP since there\nis no risk of 0.01\" accumulating. That isn't great news for \nnorthern PA where BFD only accumulated 0.85\" through all of Oct,\nand is currently on a run of 14 straight days without \nmeasurable (0.01\") rain.\n\nUpper level ridging builds back into the region for the first \nhalf of the week, helping to prolong our dry stretch and keeping\nhigh temperatures 10 to 15 degrees above normal for much of\nweek. Highs in the lower 70s are in store for all but the higher\nelevations on Wed.\n\nChances for widespread rainfall return for the second half of\nthe week. Wed night is the earliest anyone will see precip as a\ncomplex system develops over the middle of the country. There \nis still a lot of uncertainty regarding the evolution of the \nupper air pattern and phasing of energy aloft that will decide\nthe development and progression of this system. The more\nweighted/favored (per WPC) ECMWF solution yields only a brief \n12-18hr window of precip as the cold front moves through (longer\nin the eastern half of the CWA than over the west) Wed night \nand Thurs. Cold air under a sharp trough could make the first\npart of the weekend a little chilly and damp with instability \nshowers.",
